<figure class="boundary-figure consumption-figure">
  <figcaption><span class="figure-number">Figure 2</span><strong>One result, two batches, one borrower</strong></figcaption>
  <div class="consumption-route">
    <div class="borrowed-result">
      <p class="borrowed-label">Connection stays borrowed through consumption and closing</p>
      <ol>
        <li><b>Read up to 2 rows</b><div class="row-tickets"><span>101 · line 1</span><span>101 · line 2</span></div><p>Await their consumer<br/>before reading again</p></li>
        <li><b>Read up to 2 rows</b><div class="row-tickets"><span>103 · line 1</span><span>103 · line 2</span></div><p>Await their consumer<br/>before reading again</p></li>
        <li><b>Read returns no rows</b><p>End of this result</p><p>Await cursor close</p></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="returned-result"><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><b>Release</b><p>Connection can serve another request</p></div>
  </div>
  <p class="figure-note">Mina’s four order lines, ordered by order ID and line number. The two-row batches expose the boundary: awaiting consumption paces the next read. Stopping early takes the close path before release; a failure in the code below discards the client. This is a procedure illustration, not a timing or memory measurement.</p>
</figure>

